## Why This Is Asked

New angle: multi-tenant QoS in CNPA stacks, focusing on concrete enforcement rather than generic fixes. It tests how to translate policy into concrete config, tooling, and rollback plans under load.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant QoS and isolation
- PostgreSQL per-tenant pool quotas
- Kafka producer per-tenant limits and topic sharding
- HTTP backpressure and degradation strategies
- Canary rollout and per-tenant monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo middleware: per-tenant quota enforcement
function quotaCheck(req, res, next) {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const limit = getTenantQuota(tenant);
  if ((inFlight[tenant] || 0) >= limit) return res.status(429).send('Too busy');
  inFlight[tenant] = (inFlight[tenant] || 0) + 1;
  res.on('finish', () => { inFlight[tenant]--; });
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate rollback safety when quotas tighten?
- What dashboards and alerts surface per-tenant backlog and SLA breaches?

## Why This Is Asked
In CNPA stacks, authentication rotation can create bottlenecks that affect all requests; this question evaluates your ability to diagnose cross-service latency and implement effective auth caching strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed tracing across HTTP, database, Kafka, and Redis services
- JWKS caching and cache staleness trade-offs
- Resource pooling and batch processing optimization
- Canary validation and metrics-driven verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// JWKS cache example
const jwksCache = new Map();
async function getKeys(kid){
  const cached = jwksCache.get(kid);
  if (cached && !isExpired(cached)) {
    return cached.keys;
  }
  const keys = await fetchJWKS();
  jwksCache.set(kid, { keys, timestamp: Date.now() });
  return keys;
}
```
